    <briefdescription>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Plays a TTS viseme track as ARKit curve values, in step with audio playback.</para>
<para>Engine-object free so any consumer can drive it: <ref refid="class_u_tryll_lip_sync_component" kindref="compound">UTryllLipSyncComponent</ref> writes the curves to morph targets, and a MetaHuman integration can push the same curves through Epic&apos;s ARKit mapping.</para>
<para>Per turn: BeginTurn (from <ref refid="class_u_tryll_speaker_component_1a51a6b6d1559ce69cc8b31fea53f96fec" kindref="member">UTryllSpeakerComponent::OnTtsTurnStarted</ref>), then AddEvents for each viseme frame (OnTtsVisemes). Every frame: Tick with the playback clock&apos;s played samples, then read <ref refid="class_f_tryll_viseme_player_1a630c9c5aa4ad8280f3921c4202cc6a6f" kindref="member">GetCurves()</ref>.</para>
<para>What it does, as in the Unity client&apos;s TryllLipSync:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>the active viseme&apos;s pose, scaled by the event weight (loudness);</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>a smoothstep cross-fade over AttackDecayMs into each new viseme;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Intensity scales everything;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>exponential smoothing at SmoothingPerSecond, ClosureSmoothingPerSecond while p/b/m or f/v is showing (a ~70 ms closure never lands at the slow rate);</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>the track is read ahead by the delay that smoothing adds, so tuning it does not move sync;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>rest once the last shape of a turn has been held MaxHoldMs. </para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
